From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: inthewaves <inthewaves@pm.me>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 20:01:29 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Partially revert upstream "[M152] [LNP] Require
 ACCESS_LOCAL_NETWORK only when targetSdkVersion >= 37"

This partially reverts commit 2b774bd306c8dd41aa471d49141a34ef5b95beb0. It removes only the
targetSdkVersion >= 37 condition while retaining the helper and Android version check.

android.permission.ACCESS_LOCAL_NETWORK is a split permission from INTERNET. It is only implicitly
granted when

1. The app targets SDK < 37
2. The app requests INTERNET
3. The app does not already request ACCESS_LOCAL_NETWORK

However, as of commit
https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/6a6d202a1596821b35195abc88eaca7ee4ae4133, Chrome
does request ACCESS_LOCAL_NETWORK and still has targetSdkVersion 36, so this implicit grant doesn't
happen. Chrome then assumes it has local network permission, repeatedly tries to connect to the
local address, and retries until it reaches ERR_TOO_MANY_RETRIES (SIGTRAP, x0 = 0xfffffe89 = -375)

Upstream fixes this in a different way as part of an "emergency kill switch for a release blocker"
in https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/chromium/src/+/8260249 by removing
ACCESS_LOCAL_NETWORK entirely. (They also have
https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/chromium/src/+/8260580 which lets Chrome show a "too many
retries" error page instead of crashing.) However, removing ACCESS_LOCAL_NETWORK after the user has
installed a release that previously had ACCESS_LOCAL_NETWORK declared in the manifest would result
in local network still being denied.

By removing this targetSdkVersion >= 37 check, this would at least allow Vanadium to ask the user to
grant the local network permission via the Nearby devices permission group. We'll probably need to
revert https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/9672d55e2ab78ab4dd4130fb95ed8421cee9fe3f if
that commit remains, since that'll prevent Vanadium from asking the user to grant local network
permission.

Bug: 549892117, 549297724
Test: Open local network address without Nearby devices permission, see Chrome properly ask for
local network access
---
 .../permissions/PermissionUtil.java           | 19 ++++++++++---------
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

diff --git a/components/permissions/android/java/src/org/chromium/components/permissions/PermissionUtil.java b/components/permissions/android/java/src/org/chromium/components/permissions/PermissionUtil.java
index dbfa50012ccf8..ad1d9e574e6d9 100644
--- a/components/permissions/android/java/src/org/chromium/components/permissions/PermissionUtil.java
+++ b/components/permissions/android/java/src/org/chromium/components/permissions/PermissionUtil.java
@@ -13,7 +13,6 @@ import org.jni_zero.CalledByNative;
 import org.jni_zero.JniType;
 import org.jni_zero.NativeMethods;
 
-import org.chromium.base.ContextUtils;
 import org.chromium.base.metrics.RecordHistogram;
 import org.chromium.build.annotations.NullMarked;
 import org.chromium.components.content_settings.ContentSetting;
@@ -115,17 +114,19 @@ public class PermissionUtil {
     /**
      * Returns whether Local Network Access permission is required.
      *
-     * <p>Local Network Protection (LNP) runtime permission is only required when both:
-     * 1. The host device is running Android 17 (SDK 37) or higher (Build.VERSION.SDK_INT >= 37).
-     * 2. The application targets Android 17 (SDK 37) or higher (targetSdkVersion >= 37).
+     * <p>Local Network Protection (LNP) runtime permission is required when the host device is
+     * running Android 17 (SDK 37) or higher (Build.VERSION.SDK_INT >= 37).
      *
-     * <p>For apps targeting lower SDK versions (<37), local network access is implicitly granted
-     * by the OS using the INTERNET permission without requiring runtime prompts.
-     * See: https://developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/local-network-permission
+     * <p>Apps targeting lower SDK versions only receive the permission implicitly when they don't
+     * declare {@code ACCESS_LOCAL_NETWORK}. Chrome declares it, so the target SDK version does not
+     * bypass the runtime permission requirement.
+     *
+     * <p>Existing installations that ran a release declaring the permission also don't regain
+     * effective implicit access if a later release removes it: Android preserves the denied state
+     * by revoking the app op for the implicit grant.
      */
     private static boolean isLocalNetworkAccessPermissionRequired() {
-        return Build.VERSION.SDK_INT >= 37
-                && ContextUtils.getApplicationContext().getApplicationInfo().targetSdkVersion >= 37;
+        return Build.VERSION.SDK_INT >= 37;
     }
 
     /**
